## std/crypto/dudect — Welch t-test timing-leak detector
##
## ## What this module is
##
## A pure-Quartz port of dudect (Reparaz, Balasch, Verbauwhede 2017 —
## *Dude, is my code constant time?*, DATE 2017, eprint 2016/1123, ~300
## LoC C reference at https://github.com/oreparaz/dudect). The harness
## takes a sequence of `(timing_ns, class_label)` pairs from the caller
## and reports the maximum Welch t-statistic across 102 parallel t-tests
## (1 uncropped + 100 cropped at exponentially-spaced upper percentiles
## + 1 second-order test on `(x - μ_uncropped)²`). The maximum |t| is
## the leak score:
##
## | `max |t|` | interpretation                                        |
## |-----------|-------------------------------------------------------|
## | `< 5`     | no leak detected (Reparaz §4 practitioner cutoff)     |
## | `5..10`   | possible leak, borderline                             |
## | `10..100` | likely a leak                                         |
## | `≥ 100`   | overwhelming evidence of a leak (Reparaz §4)          |
##
## ## Why a stats-only API (no fn-callback)
##
## The harness is a streaming statistics library. **The caller drives the
## measurement loop** — fills the input buffer, calls the target, times
## with `time_mono_ns()`, calls `dudect_push(ctx, ns, class)`. Two
## upsides over Reparaz's fn-callback shape:
##
##   1. No cross-module `Fn` value passing — avoids HB-DOG.12-class
##      compiler hazards.
##   2. Maximally flexible — caller can shape inputs however needed
##      (fixed-vs-random, all-zeros-vs-all-ones, scalar bit toggle, etc).
##      The harness only needs the (timing, class) pairs.
##
## ## Two-class measurement protocol (the dudect convention)
##
## Class 0 = "fixed input" — the same byte pattern every call (typically
## all-zeros, or a chosen edge case). Class 1 = "random input" — fresh
## random bytes per call. Any timing dependence on input data shows up
## as `μ_class_0 ≠ μ_class_1`. The Welch t-test detects this with no
## distribution assumption on the timing noise (just that both classes
## see the same OS preemption / cache-line / branch-predictor noise
## floor, which they do because they're interleaved within one run).
##
## The caller is responsible for class labeling. The canonical pattern:
##
## ```quartz
##   var ctx = dudect_new()
##   var i = 0
##   while i < N
##     var class = i % 2
##     fill_input(buf, class)        # caller's responsibility
##     var t0 = time_mono_ns()
##     target_under_test(buf)
##     var t1 = time_mono_ns()
##     dudect_push(ctx, t1 - t0, class)
##     i = i + 1
##   end
##   dudect_assert_ct("target", ctx, DUDECT_T_THRESHOLD_LEAK)
## ```
##
## ## Time source (v1 = `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)`)
##
## `time_mono_ns()` resolves to nanoseconds via `clock_gettime` on macOS
## and Linux. Syscall overhead is ~20-50 ns in practice; the canonical
## dudect reference uses `__rdtsc` for ~0.3 ns/cycle resolution. We ship
## v1 on `time_mono_ns()` because (a) the canary test produces a leak
## signal large enough for clock_gettime to see, and (b) `rdtsc` would
## need a new compiler intrinsic. If a real-primitive test needs finer
## resolution than v1 provides, file `@rdtsc` (~30 LoC in
## `codegen_runtime.qz` + 1 line in `intrinsic_registry.qz` per the
## "Adding a New Builtin" 4-step recipe in CLAUDE.md). The design memo
## (`docs/design/SECRET_T_AND_DUDECT_DESIGN.md` §Layer 2 "Implementation
## outline") flags this as the v1→v2 trigger.
##
## ## Algorithm — the 102 parallel t-tests
##
## After collection of N raw measurements:
##
##   1. Drop the first 10 samples (first-call cold-cache noise; matches
##      Reparaz's `DUDECT_DROP_FIRST_N`).
##   2. Sort a copy of the timings ascending (quicksort, in-place on the
##      copy — `Vec<Int>` of N items; N up to ~100K is fine).
##   3. Compute 100 upper-tail percentile cutoffs by the exponential
##      schedule `1 - 0.5^(i/10)` for `i ∈ 1..100`. This concentrates the
##      crop thresholds near the upper tail (i=10 ≈ p50, i=50 ≈ p96.9,
##      i=100 ≈ p99.9) — empirically Reparaz et al. found this multi-
##      cutoff fanout finds leaks that any single percentile misses.
##   4. Initialize 102 fresh Welford accumulators (`DudectTest`).
##   5. Single pass over the N - 10 retained measurements:
##      - test[0] = uncropped — push every measurement.
##      - test[1..100] = cropped — push iff `measurement < cutoff[i-1]`.
##      - test[101] = second-order — push `(x - μ_uncropped[class])²`,
##        the running mean from test[0]. Catches variance leaks that
##        the mean-difference tests miss.
##   6. Compute Welch t for each of the 102 accumulators, return max |t|.
##
## Welch's t formula (two-sample, unequal-variance):
##
##   `t = (μ_a − μ_b) / sqrt(σ²_a / n_a + σ²_b / n_b)`
##
## Welford online variance (Knuth TAOCP vol 2 §4.2.2, also Welford 1962):
##
##   `n += 1; δ = x − μ; μ += δ/n; m2 += δ * (x − μ);  σ² = m2 / (n-1)`
##
## ## Recommended N
##
## - Canary test: 10K-30K samples. Leaky early-exit will hit |t|≥100
##   in a few thousand samples; the canary's correctness signal is fast.
## - Real-primitive validation: 30K-100K samples is the dudect-canonical
##   "I'm done" point if max|t| stays under 5. Constant-time validation
##   of e.g. dalek's curve25519 took millions; we don't need that for
##   the v1 stdlib-correctness story.
##
## See `docs/design/SECRET_T_AND_DUDECT_DESIGN.md` §Layer 2 for the full
## design. See `spec/qz/dudect_canary_smoke.qz` for the leaky-vs-honest
## canary. See `spec/qz/dudect_smoke.qz` for real-primitive coverage.

import * from ffi/socket  # malloc/free/memcpy/memset for buffer allocation

# =============================================================================
# Tunables
# =============================================================================

const DUDECT_NUMBER_PERCENTILES = 100
const DUDECT_NUMBER_TESTS = 102            # 1 uncropped + 100 cropped + 1 2nd-order
const DUDECT_DROP_FIRST_N = 10             # drop first-call cold-cache outliers

## Welch |t| ≥ this fails `dudect_assert_ct`. Practitioner cutoff from
## Reparaz et al. §4: "the threshold is set to 5 based on practical
## experience." Cross-module-exportable as a def returning F64 because
## module-level `var X: F64 = ...` does not currently propagate the
## binding across the qzmod import boundary (callers see QZ0403).
def dudect_t_threshold_leak(): F64 = 5.0

## |t| ≥ this constitutes "overwhelming evidence of a leak" per Reparaz
## et al. §4. The canary test (deliberately-leaky early-exit `ct_eq_bytes`)
## must hit this to validate the harness itself.
def dudect_t_threshold_overwhelming(): F64 = 100.0

# =============================================================================
# Per-test Welford accumulator
# =============================================================================

## One of 102 t-test slots. Holds streaming Welford state for both
## classes — n, mean, and m2 (sum of squared deviations from the
## running mean). σ² = m2 / (n - 1) is computed on demand at scoring
## time.
struct DudectTest
  n0: Int          # samples seen for class 0
  n1: Int          # samples seen for class 1
  mean0: F64       # running mean for class 0
  mean1: F64       # running mean for class 1
  m2_0: F64        # Welford m2 for class 0
  m2_1: F64        # Welford m2 for class 1
end

## Harness state. Raw timings + class labels are accumulated as the
## caller drives the measurement loop; the 102 parallel t-tests are
## computed at scoring time (`dudect_compute_max_t`).
struct DudectCtx
  timings: Vec<Int>    # raw measurements, ns
  classes: Vec<Int>    # parallel; 0 = class A (fixed), 1 = class B (random)
end

## Allocate a fresh harness context. Caller drives the measurement loop
## then calls `dudect_compute_max_t` (returns the score) or
## `dudect_assert_ct` (returns the score AND fails on leak).
def dudect_new(): DudectCtx
  return DudectCtx {
    timings: vec_new<Int>(),
    classes: vec_new<Int>(),
  }
end

## Record one measurement. `ns` is a duration in nanoseconds (typically
## from `time_mono_ns()` deltas); `class` is 0 or 1 (the dudect-canonical
## "fixed input" vs "random input" labeling).
def dudect_push(ctx: DudectCtx, ns: Int, class: Int): Void
  ctx.timings.push(ns)
  ctx.classes.push(class)
end

## Total measurements pushed so far.
def dudect_n(ctx: DudectCtx): Int = ctx.timings.size

# =============================================================================
# Sort — quicksort over Vec<Int>
# =============================================================================

# `bench.qz`'s insertion sort is O(N²) — fine for ≤30 samples, way too
# slow for the 10K+ samples a real dudect run wants. Recursive 3-way
# Lomuto-ish quicksort works at scale; pivot is the middle element to
# avoid worst-case on already-sorted input. We don't move this to
# `std/sort.qz` because no second consumer exists yet — premature
# factoring trades a real follow-up for a speculative one.
def _dudect_qs(a: Vec<Int>, lo: Int, hi: Int): Void
  if lo >= hi
    return
  end
  var pivot = a[(lo + hi) / 2]
  var i = lo
  var j = hi
  while i <= j
    while a[i] < pivot
      i = i + 1
    end
    while a[j] > pivot
      j = j - 1
    end
    if i <= j
      var t = a[i]
      a[i] = a[j]
      a[j] = t
      i = i + 1
      j = j - 1
    end
  end
  _dudect_qs(a, lo, j)
  _dudect_qs(a, i, hi)
end

def _dudect_sort_copy(src: Vec<Int>): Vec<Int>
  var copy = vec_new<Int>()
  var i = 0
  while i < src.size
    copy.push(src[i])
    i = i + 1
  end
  if copy.size > 1
    _dudect_qs(copy, 0, copy.size - 1)
  end
  return copy
end

# =============================================================================
# Percentile cutoffs — Reparaz exponential schedule
# =============================================================================

# 100 thresholds from the sorted timings, exponentially spaced toward the
# upper tail: `cutoff[i] = sorted[floor((1 − 0.5^(i/10)) * n)]` for
# `i ∈ 1..100`. Matches the C reference's
# `percentile(exec_times, 1 − pow(0.5, 10*(i+1)/100), n)` literally.
# Concentrates outlier-cropping near the tail where OS-preemption
# samples land, leaving the bulk of the distribution intact for the
# mean-difference test.
def _dudect_percentiles(sorted: Vec<Int>): Vec<Int>
  var out = vec_new<Int>()
  var n = sorted.size
  if n == 0
    return out
  end
  var i = 1
  while i <= DUDECT_NUMBER_PERCENTILES
    # 1 - 0.5^(i/10). Note: i=10 → 0.5, i=50 → ~0.969, i=100 → ~0.999.
    var frac: F64 = to_f64(i) / 10.0
    var p: F64 = 1.0 - f64_pow(0.5, frac)
    var idx = to_int(p * to_f64(n))
    if idx >= n
      idx = n - 1
    end
    if idx < 0
      idx = 0
    end
    out.push(sorted[idx])
    i = i + 1
  end
  return out
end

# =============================================================================
# Welford update + t-statistic
# =============================================================================

# Streaming update of one DudectTest's per-class Welford state. Vec-of-
# struct uses Quartz value semantics, so we read the slot, mutate the
# local, write it back. Same shape as `_replace_peer` in wireguard's
# device.qz.
def _dudect_test_push(tests: Vec<DudectTest>, k: Int, x: F64, class: Int): Void
  var t = tests[k]
  if class == 0
    t.n0 = t.n0 + 1
    var delta = x - t.mean0
    t.mean0 = t.mean0 + delta / to_f64(t.n0)
    t.m2_0 = t.m2_0 + delta * (x - t.mean0)
  end
  if class != 0
    t.n1 = t.n1 + 1
    var delta = x - t.mean1
    t.mean1 = t.mean1 + delta / to_f64(t.n1)
    t.m2_1 = t.m2_1 + delta * (x - t.mean1)
  end
  tests[k] = t
end

# Welch's two-sample unequal-variance t-statistic. Both classes need
# at least 2 samples for variance to be defined; otherwise return 0
# (don't accuse the target of leaking when we just haven't collected
# enough). Denominator-zero (constant-output edge case) also returns 0.
def _dudect_t_value(t: DudectTest): F64
  if t.n0 < 2
    return 0.0
  end
  if t.n1 < 2
    return 0.0
  end
  var var0: F64 = t.m2_0 / to_f64(t.n0 - 1)
  var var1: F64 = t.m2_1 / to_f64(t.n1 - 1)
  var num: F64 = t.mean0 - t.mean1
  var denom: F64 = f64_sqrt(var0 / to_f64(t.n0) + var1 / to_f64(t.n1))
  if denom == 0.0
    return 0.0
  end
  return num / denom
end

def _dudect_abs(x: F64): F64
  if x < 0.0
    return 0.0 - x
  end
  return x
end

# =============================================================================
# Public scoring API
# =============================================================================

## Score the harness — runs the 102 parallel t-tests over accumulated
## measurements and returns `max |t|` across them. The leak score.
##
## Returns `0.0` if fewer than 100 samples accumulated (not enough for
## the percentile machinery; caller's loop count is too small).
##
## Side-effect-free; safe to call repeatedly. Cost is O(N log N) sort
## + O(N * 100) per-percentile gating + O(N) Welford updates. For
## N=10K that's roughly 10ms in practice on modern hardware.
def dudect_compute_max_t(ctx: DudectCtx): F64
  var n = ctx.timings.size
  if n < 100
    return 0.0
  end

  # Step 1: sort a copy + compute 100 percentile cutoffs.
  var sorted = _dudect_sort_copy(ctx.timings)
  var pcts = _dudect_percentiles(sorted)

  # Step 2: 102 fresh Welford accumulators.
  var tests = vec_new<DudectTest>()
  var k = 0
  while k < DUDECT_NUMBER_TESTS
    tests.push(DudectTest {
      n0: 0,
      n1: 0,
      mean0: 0.0,
      mean1: 0.0,
      m2_0: 0.0,
      m2_1: 0.0,
    })
    k = k + 1
  end

  # Step 3: single pass — push every measurement into 1 uncropped
  # accumulator, 0..100 cropped accumulators (gated on percentile),
  # and 1 second-order accumulator on (x - μ_uncropped[class])².
  var i = DUDECT_DROP_FIRST_N
  while i < n
    var x: Int = ctx.timings[i]
    var c: Int = ctx.classes[i]
    var xf: F64 = to_f64(x)

    # test[0] = uncropped.
    _dudect_test_push(tests, 0, xf, c)

    # test[1..100] = cropped at percentiles[0..99]. Slot 1+p uses
    # cutoff p, so cutoff[0] is the i=1 (least-aggressive) crop and
    # cutoff[99] is the i=100 (most-aggressive, ~p99.9) crop.
    var p = 0
    while p < DUDECT_NUMBER_PERCENTILES
      if x < pcts[p]
        _dudect_test_push(tests, 1 + p, xf, c)
      end
      p = p + 1
    end

    # test[101] = second-order on (x - μ_uncropped[class])². Uses the
    # running mean from test[0] *as observed at this point in the pass*.
    # Matches Reparaz's single-pass formulation; the small lag converges
    # quickly and doesn't affect leak detection materially.
    var mu_slot = tests[0]
    var center: F64 = 0.0
    if c == 0
      center = mu_slot.mean0
    end
    if c != 0
      center = mu_slot.mean1
    end
    var d: F64 = xf - center
    _dudect_test_push(tests, DUDECT_NUMBER_TESTS - 1, d * d, c)

    i = i + 1
  end

  # Step 4: scan, return max |t|.
  var max_t: F64 = 0.0
  var kk = 0
  while kk < DUDECT_NUMBER_TESTS
    var tv = _dudect_t_value(tests[kk])
    var av = _dudect_abs(tv)
    if av > max_t
      max_t = av
    end
    kk = kk + 1
  end
  return max_t
end

## Assert constant-time. Computes `max |t|`, prints a one-line
## PASS/FAIL summary, and returns 1 (true, "leak detected") if the
## threshold is crossed, 0 otherwise.
##
## Doesn't `panic` — smoke-test drivers (canonical pattern in
## `spec/qz/*_smoke.qz`) accumulate a pass/fail counter and `return 1`
## from `main` on any failure. The returned Int composes with that.
def dudect_assert_ct(name: String, ctx: DudectCtx, threshold: F64): Int
  var max_t = dudect_compute_max_t(ctx)
  var av = _dudect_abs(max_t)
  var n = ctx.timings.size

  if av >= threshold
    puts("dudect FAIL [" + name + "]: max|t| = " + f64_to_str(av) +
         "  threshold = " + f64_to_str(threshold) +
         "  n = " + to_str(n))
    return 1
  end

  puts("dudect PASS [" + name + "]: max|t| = " + f64_to_str(av) +
       "  threshold = " + f64_to_str(threshold) +
       "  n = " + to_str(n))
  return 0
end

## The inverse — asserts the harness DETECTED a leak. Used by the
## canary test to verify the harness itself is working. Returns 1 if
## the expected leak was NOT detected (canary failure), 0 if the
## expected leak WAS detected (canary passed).
def dudect_assert_leak(name: String, ctx: DudectCtx, expected_threshold: F64): Int
  var max_t = dudect_compute_max_t(ctx)
  var av = _dudect_abs(max_t)
  var n = ctx.timings.size

  if av < expected_threshold
    puts("dudect CANARY FAIL [" + name +
         "]: expected max|t| >= " + f64_to_str(expected_threshold) +
         "  got " + f64_to_str(av) +
         "  n = " + to_str(n))
    return 1
  end

  puts("dudect CANARY PASS [" + name +
       "]: max|t| = " + f64_to_str(av) +
       "  (>= " + f64_to_str(expected_threshold) + ")" +
       "  n = " + to_str(n))
  return 0
end

# =============================================================================
# xorshift64 — tiny deterministic PRNG for harness-driven input generation
# =============================================================================
#
# Crypto code in `std/crypto/rng.qz` uses HKDF-BLAKE2s-over-OS-entropy; that's
# the right choice for production keys. For dudect-style timing experiments
# we want deterministic input generation so canary results are reproducible
# across runs. xorshift64 (Marsaglia 2003) is ~20 ns/call and good enough
# entropy for "fill a buffer with bytes that aren't all the same."
#
# DO NOT USE for cryptographic randomness — that's `std/crypto/rng.qz`.

struct DudectRng
  state: Int
end

## Construct a deterministic PRNG. Pass a non-zero seed.
def dudect_rng_new(seed: Int): DudectRng
  var s = seed
  if s == 0
    s = 1   # xorshift64 dies at 0; remap to 1.
  end
  return DudectRng { state: s }
end

## Pull the next 64-bit sample from xorshift64. Marsaglia constants
## (13, 7, 17) — the classic combination.
def dudect_rng_next(r: DudectRng): Int
  var x = r.state
  x = x ^ (x << 13)
  x = x ^ ((x >> 7) & 144115188075855871)   # 2^57 - 1 mask: simulate logical shift right by 7 on signed Int
  x = x ^ (x << 17)
  r.state = x
  return x
end

## Fill `len` bytes at `buf` with pseudo-random data from `r`.
def dudect_rng_fill(r: DudectRng, buf: Int, len: Int): Void
  var i = 0
  while i + 8 <= len
    var v = dudect_rng_next(r)
    var k = 0
    while k < 8
      ptr_write_byte(buf + i + k, (v >> (k * 8)) & 255)
      k = k + 1
    end
    i = i + 8
  end
  if i < len
    var v = dudect_rng_next(r)
    var k = 0
    while i + k < len
      ptr_write_byte(buf + i + k, (v >> (k * 8)) & 255)
      k = k + 1
    end
  end
end
